## [GAP ANALYSIS] ECDSA test is a self round-trip and does not check interoperability of the generated DER

**Severity:** LOW - the hand-written DER encoder (used for ES* JWS handling) is not checked against the JCA

### The Problem

```java
byte[] rsConcat = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(sign, ...);
byte[] asn1Des = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().concatenatedRSToASN1DER(rsConcat, ...);
byte[] rsConcat2 = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().asn1derToConcatenatedRS(asn1Des, ...);
Assert.assertArrayEquals(rsConcat, rsConcat2);
```

`asn1Des` is only fed back into this PR's own `ASN1Decoder`. It is never compared with `sign` and never passed to `Signature.verify`. A bug that the encoder and decoder share would still pass. One example is a non-DER length encoding in the long form, which ES512 needs because its DER signature is about 137-139 bytes and so is longer than 127. Another is a wrong tag that both sides agree on.

### Theory / Proof

For ECDSA, JCA's `Signature.sign()` emits canonical DER (`SEQUENCE { INTEGER r, INTEGER s }` with minimal two's-complement integers, per X.690 / RFC 3279 section 2.2.3). `BigInteger.toByteArray()` also yields a minimal two's-complement encoding. So a correct encoder must reproduce `sign` byte-for-byte, and the check is deterministic. `ECDSAAlgorithm.concatenatedRSToASN1DER` is what `ECDSASignatureVerifierContext` feeds into `Signature.verify` when a provider verifies ES256/384/512 tokens, so interoperability is the property that matters.

### What happens today vs. what will break

The current encoder looks right, so the test passes. A future change to `ASN1Encoder.writeLength` or `ASN1Decoder.readLength` that stays symmetric but is not DER would go unnoticed until a real verification fails.

### Recommendation

```java
byte[] asn1Des = prov.getEcdsaCryptoProvider().concatenatedRSToASN1DER(rsConcat, algorithm.getSignatureLength());
Assert.assertArrayEquals(sign, asn1Des);

Signature verifier = Signature.getInstance(JavaAlgorithm.getJavaAlgorithm(algorithm.name()));
verifier.initVerify(keyPair.getPublic());
verifier.update(data);
Assert.assertTrue(verifier.verify(asn1Des));
```

Also generate the key pair with the matching curve per algorithm (`secp256r1`/`secp384r1`/`secp521r1`). `KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC")` defaults to P-256, so today `testES512` signs with a P-256 key and its DER is under 128 bytes. That means the long-form length code in `ASN1Encoder.writeLength` / `ASN1Decoder.readLength` is never exercised by any test:

```java
KeyPairGenerator kpg = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC");
kpg.initialize(new ECGenParameterSpec(curveFor(algorithm))); // secp256r1 / secp384r1 / secp521r1
```

